  faqH: 'Frequently asked questions',
  faq: [
    { q: 'Is Al-Ahsa worth visiting?', a: 'Absolutely. Al-Ahsa is the world’s largest oasis (Guinness 2020) and a UNESCO World Heritage Site (2018). It pairs natural wonders — Al-Qarah Mountain’s caves, Al-Asfar Lake and the freshwater springs — with living heritage in Hofuf: Qasr Ibrahim, Qaisariyah Souq and Jawatha Mosque, plus palm groves, oasis fruits and distinctive local cuisine. A welcoming, family-friendly destination blending history, nature and hospitality.' },
    { q: 'What are the best places to visit in Al-Ahsa?', a: 'Highlights include Al-Qarah Mountain with its caves and the “Land of Civilisations” centre, the historic Qasr Ibrahim, the heritage Qaisariyah Souq, Jawatha Mosque (one of the earliest mosques in Islam), Al-Asfar Lake, and Qasr Sahood in Al-Mubarraz. Browse the Attractions page for the full list you can add to “My Trip”.' },
    { q: 'What are the opening hours for Al-Qarah Mountain?', a: `It opens daily from 8:00 am to 10:00 pm (hours may be extended on holidays), and admission is SAR 50 for adults, booked through the “Land of Civilisations” centre. Hours can change seasonally, so confirm before you go. (Reviewed ${rev})` },
    { q: 'When does Qaisariyah Souq open?', a: `The souq trades in the morning from 8 am to 12 noon and in the evening from 3:30 pm to 10 pm (Friday evenings only), and entry is free. Evenings are the best time, when the shops open and the market comes alive. (Reviewed ${rev})` },
    { q: 'When is the best time to visit Al-Ahsa?', a: 'A year-round destination; the weather is gentlest from October to March — ideal for the landmarks and forts — while summer brings fresh dates, evening experiences and the parks. Early in the year is the season of the Dates Festival.' },
    { q: 'How do I travel from Riyadh to Al-Ahsa?', a: 'Al-Ahsa is about 326 km from Riyadh. The fastest and most comfortable option is the train to Hofuf station (around two hours and ten minutes with SAR), or driving via the highway (around 3 hours 20 minutes), or flying into Al-Ahsa International Airport.' },
    { q: 'What is the distance from Al-Ahsa to Bahrain and Dammam?', a: 'Al-Ahsa is close to Dammam and Khobar via a good highway network (Dammam is roughly 150 km, about 1.5 hours). Bahrain is reached by road across the King Fahd Causeway via Khobar (about two hours). SAR trains also stop at nearby Abqaiq (Buqayq) on the same line.' },
    { q: 'How do I book a Riyadh–Al-Ahsa train ticket?', a: 'Book through Saudi Arabia Railways (SAR) via its official app or website sar.com.sa, or at station ticket counters. The line connects Riyadh with Hofuf and Dammam; book early during holidays and peak seasons.' },
    { q: 'What is the postal code for Al-Ahsa?', a: 'Al-Ahsa (Hofuf and Al-Mubarraz) falls within postal codes beginning with 36, with the exact code varying by district and location. For a precise address, use the Saudi Post (SPL) National Address directory at splonline.com.sa.' },
    { q: 'What are the best hotels to stay at in Al-Ahsa?', a: 'Al-Ahsa offers modern hotels and resorts across Hofuf and Al-Mubarraz, alongside heritage inns, chalets and farm stays for an authentic local experience among the palms. To find the best fit for your budget and location, compare options and reviews on trusted booking platforms. (We don’t endorse specific venues, to keep the information neutral.)' },
    { q: 'What is Al-Ahsa famous for?', a: 'Al-Ahsa is famous for its golden Khalas dates (its best-known variety), Hasawi red rice and folk dishes such as harees, balaleet and date bread (also called red bread). Its oasis fruits include the Hasawi fig, lomi, kanar and Suwari pomegranate. In crafts, the Hasawi bisht embroidered with zari thread stands out, along with pottery, palm-frond ware, incense and perfumes from Qaisariyah Souq.' },
    { q: 'How did Al-Ahsa get its name?', a: '“Al-Ahsa” is the plural of “hisy”: the sweet water that runs and gathers beneath a thin layer of sand, which springs up as soon as your hand uncovers it. It is a precise description of this land — a generous oasis of springs, channels and groundwater that made it the largest oasis in the world.' },
    { q: 'What are the best traditional restaurants in Al-Ahsa?', a: 'Hasawi breakfasts and folk dishes (Hasawi red rice, balaleet, harees, date bread) are served at several heritage restaurants in Hofuf, and some farms offer a Hasawi breakfast in nature. For the best current pick, check up-to-date local reviews. (We don’t endorse specific restaurants, to keep the information neutral.)' },
